WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.699
Here's a slice through a real wing.

00:00:02.699 --> 00:00:10.720
It's called a cross section: imagine cutting the wing like a loaf of bread, and looking at the cut face.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.960
It has a thin outer skin, two spars that run along the wing, and a row of small stiffeners called stringers along the top and the bottom.

00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:30.448
To find the stresses in it, you'd need the area and position of every one of these little shapes.

00:00:30.448 --> 00:00:32.733
That's a lot of bookkeeping.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:42.260
So in this lesson, we'll learn a way to simplify the section, and then use it to solve both of your homework problems.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:48.887
Let's name the parts properly.

00:00:48.887 --> 00:00:52.919
A spar is like an I beam running along the wing.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:57.287
The tall thin sheet in the middle is called the web.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.753
The thick strips at the top and bottom of the web are called flanges.

00:01:02.753 --> 00:01:06.040
They're where the spar connects to the skin.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:10.708
The box made by the two spars and the skin between them is called the wing box.

00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:12.687
It does most of the structural work.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.097
Lift pushes the wing up.

00:01:15.097 --> 00:01:24.360
That does three things to the wing box: it bends it, it pushes on it with a shear force, and it twists it.

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:28.281
Bending makes stresses along the span, into the screen.

00:01:28.281 --> 00:01:34.287
Shear forces and twisting make stresses that run around the section, in the skin and the webs.

00:01:36.698 --> 00:01:41.378
Bending produces direct stress: stress that points along the span.

00:01:41.378 --> 00:01:42.938
We'll call it sigma z.

00:01:44.831 --> 00:01:52.282
Above the neutral axis the material is stretched, and below it the material is squeezed.

00:01:52.282 --> 00:01:56.431
The farther from the axis, the bigger the stress.

00:01:58.565 --> 00:02:09.818
The formula, for bending about the horizontal axis, is sigma z equals M x over I x x, times y.

00:02:09.818 --> 00:02:15.085
Here y is the height above the neutral axis.

00:02:16.098 --> 00:02:20.968
I x x measures how much area sits far from the axis.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:28.178
For small pieces, it's just each area times its height squared, all added up.

00:02:31.865 --> 00:02:37.353
Shear forces and twisting create shear stress in the thin walls.

00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:38.897
We'll call it tau.

00:02:38.897 --> 00:02:42.585
It acts along the wall, around the section.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:52.383
The walls are so thin that tau is nearly constant through the thickness.

00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:57.565
So instead of tau, we usually track tau times the thickness.

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:04.921
That product is called the shear flow, q.

00:03:04.921 --> 00:03:09.232
It's a force per unit length along the wall, in newtons per millimeter.

00:03:12.598 --> 00:03:13.517
Why bother?

00:03:13.517 --> 00:03:19.702
Where walls meet, the shear flows add up like water flowing through pipes.

00:03:19.702 --> 00:03:22.878
That makes them easy to keep track of.

00:03:26.898 --> 00:03:34.338
To get back to stress at the end, just divide by the thickness: tau equals q over t.
